# Working agreement

This is a field-facing tool for mobile/field hospitals. Lives may depend on its output. Treat every change with that seriousness.

## Source of truth
- You (Cursor) have the full repository. You are the source of truth for what the code currently contains. Do not guess at file contents — read them.
- When asked a question about the code, read the relevant files in full before answering. Do not answer from a partial read or from memory of an earlier pass. "I've read enough" is not acceptable.
- Read the actual source before claiming anything about it. If you have not verified a claim against the file, say so.

## Read-only vs. authorized changes
- Treat a request as **read-only** unless it explicitly authorizes changes. Diagnosis, inspection, "where is…", "how does…", "assess…", "what would it take to…" are read-only. Report findings; do not edit.
- Only make edits when the request explicitly authorizes them (e.g. "make the change," "implement," "fix it").
- State at the top of your response whether you treated the task as read-only or as an authorized change.

## One thing at a time
- Do exactly the task asked. Complete it before moving on.
- Do not bundle unrelated work into a change. If you notice something else that needs attention, **flag it and move on** — do not fix it in the same pass.
- Do not "tidy," refactor, rename, or reformat code outside the scope of the request, even if it looks improvable.

## Scope discipline
- This is a single-file application (`Portable Solution Site Mapping Tool.html`). Do not split it into multiple files, introduce a build step, add a framework, or add dependencies unless explicitly asked.
- Prefer the simplest change that correctly does the job. This tool serves stressed users in high-pressure environments; complexity is a cost.

## Design decisions belong to the developer
- When there is a real choice, present the options clearly and concisely, state a recommendation, and stop. Do not pick for the developer.
- Once the developer has decided, implement that decision. Do not relitigate it.

## Reporting
- Be precise and concise. Put the key question or decision at the end, clearly separated.
- When you are wrong or have made a mistake, say so plainly, correct it, and move on. Do not pad or hedge.
- Do not explain away a visual observation the developer reports. If they say something looks wrong, investigate it — do not rationalize it.

## Git
- Do not run commits or pushes. The developer handles Git.
- You may propose a commit message when a unit of work is complete.
